[% algorithm_vocab([20, 'liela', 'strādā']) %]

<h2>Design Notes</h2>

<p>
The algorithm is based on Kārlis Krēsliņš, <i>A stemming algorithm for
Latvian</i>, PhD thesis, Loughborough University, 1996, and on the light
form of it in Lucene's <code>LatvianStemmer</code>, which removes one
noun or adjective ending and undoes some consonant alternations.  The
Snowball stemmer starts from the light form and adds verbs, participles
and the definite and comparative suffixes of adjectives, and keeps the
forms of a diminutive together.
Each addition was measured on the lemmas of the UD Latvian-LVTB treebank
(see <a href="#evaluation">Evaluation</a>) and kept only if it merged
forms of one word without merging too many different words.  The intended
use is information retrieval, so a rule which merged a word with its
opposite or with an unrelated word was dropped even when it scored well.
</p>

<p>
The differences from Krēsliņš are: one inflectional ending is removed
(the longest that leaves a valid stem) rather than a cascade of
first-match lists; the size checks are regions rather than the Porter
measure; there is no stopword list; the type vowel of a verb is removed
with its ending (so <i>strādāt</i> and <i>strādāju</i> meet); his
derivational suffix steps are not included; and <i>ļ ņ č š ž</i> are
undone at the end of the stem, which he leaves alone as ambiguous (the
exception lists below handle the cases where that goes wrong).  The
difference from Lucene, apart from what is added, is that Lucene allows
<i>-ie</i> to leave a stem with no vowel, while here it follows the same
rule as the other endings.
</p>

<h3>Letters and regions</h3>

<p>
The vowels are <i>a ā e ē i ī o u ū</i>.  The type vowels of verbs are
<i>ā ē ī o</i> (<i>strādāt</i>, <i>redzēt</i>, <i>skatīt</i>,
<i>darboties</i>).  None of the single-byte encodings Snowball supports
has all the Latvian letters, so the stemmer is UTF-8 only.  It expects lower
case input.
</p>

<p>
<i>p1</i> is the position after the first vowel, but at least 3 letters
into the word: a suffix in <i>p1</i> leaves a stem of at least 3 letters
with a vowel.  This is the condition of the light stemmer, and of all the
noun endings.  It keeps short words such as <i>ola</i>, <i>sula</i>,
<i>tēja</i> intact enough to be found.
</p>

<p>
<i>p2</i> is the same, but the vowel must come after the negation prefix
<i>ne-</i> and a verb prefix (<i>aiz- ap- at- ie- iz- no- pa- pār- pie-
sa- uz-</i>), if the word has them.  It is used for the verb endings and
for every suffix rule.  Without it short verbs lose their whole root:
<i>aizies</i> would become the preposition <i>aiz</i>, <i>nostājas</i> the
adverb <i>nost</i>, <i>pienāk</i> would meet <i>piens</i>.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: ending</h3>

<p>
First a verb ending is tried, with the conditions given; if there is none,
a noun or adjective ending in <i>p1</i>.  A verb ending is always longer
than any noun ending of the same word, so this is the same as taking the
longest ending.  After a verb ending, a type vowel before it is removed
too (if the rest is in <i>p2</i>): <i>strādāt</i>, <i>strādāšu</i>,
<i>strādāsiet</i> &rarr; <i>strād</i>; <i>skatīties</i> &rarr;
<i>skat</i>.
</p>

<dl>
<dt><i>-t</i> (infinitive)
<dd>if the stem has two vowels.  One-syllable infinitives (<i>nest</i>,
<i>sākt</i>, <i>ņemt</i>) keep their <i>-t</i>: removing it as well gained
almost nothing on the tuning lemmas and was worse on the held-out ones.
<dt><i>-šu -si -sim -siet</i> (future)
<dd>only after a type vowel: <i>strādāšu</i>, but <i>-šu</i> is also the
genitive plural of many nouns (<i>sieviešu</i>, <i>interešu</i>).
<i>-šu</i> is not removed after <i>ī</i> either: <i>-īšu</i> is the
genitive plural of the diminutives in <i>-ītis</i> and <i>-īte</i>
(<i>brālīšu</i>), and the future <i>darīšu</i> is left as <i>darīt</i>.
<dt><i>-ētu</i> (conditional)
<dd><i>-ātu</i>, <i>-ītu</i>, <i>-otu</i> are not removed: they are also
accusatives of common nouns (<i>rezultātu</i>, <i>kredītu</i>,
<i>pilotu</i>).
<dt><i>-ties</i>
<dd>not after <i>n</i>: <i>centies</i> is <i>cent-ies</i> (from
<i>censties</i>), and <i>cen-ties</i> would meet <i>cena</i>.
<dt><i>-iet -ies -aties -oties -amies -āmies -dams -dama -damas -damās
-damies -usi -usies -ušies</i>
<dd>in <i>p2</i>.
</dl>

<p>
The noun and adjective endings are those of the light stemmer (case,
number, gender, definiteness): <i>-ajiem -ajai -ajam -ajām -ajos -ajās
-ajā -iem -ais -ai -ei -ām -am -ēm -īm -im -um -us -as -ās -es -os -ij -īs
-ēs -is -ie -u -a -i -e -ā -ē -ī -ū -o -s -š</i>.  Which ending was
removed matters later: <i>-a -i -u -us -os -iem</i> are the endings after
which the consonant alternations of declensions II, V and VI appear.
</p>

<h3>Step 2: definite and comparative adjectives</h3>

<p>
<i>-aj-</i> of the definite adjective is removed if in <i>p2</i>.  Most
definite endings are already removed whole in step 1 (<i>pirmajiem</i>
&rarr; <i>pirm</i>), so this only catches forms such as <i>pirmaja</i>
written without the long vowel; it also shortens nouns in <i>-ajs</i>
(<i>samurajs</i>, <i>tramvajs</i>), but the same way in all their forms.  Then <i>-āk-</i> of the comparative
(<i>lielāks</i>, <i>lielākā</i>, <i>labāk</i> &rarr; <i>liel</i>,
<i>lab</i>), unless the word is <i>vecāk-</i>: <i>vecāki</i> (parents) is a noun.  If a comparative
was removed and the word starts with <i>vis-</i>, the superlative prefix
is removed too, provided 3 letters remain (<i>vislielākais</i> &rarr;
<i>liel</i>), and the regions are worked out again for the rest.
</p>

<p>
The <i>-āk-</i> of <i>nākt</i> and <i>sākt</i> (<i>pienāk</i>,
<i>iesākas</i>) is safe because of <i>p2</i>.
</p>

<h3>Step 3: diminutives</h3>

<p>
Diminutives are not reduced to their base word: they often name a
different thing (<i>riekstiņi</i> are biscuits, not <i>rieksti</i>, nuts;
<i>sieriņš</i> is a curd snack, not <i>siers</i>, cheese; <i>desiņas</i>
are frankfurters), and the evaluation data gives them their own lemma.
What this step does is keep the forms of a diminutive together.
</p>

<dl>
<dt><i>-iņ-</i>
<dd>if in <i>p1</i> and after a consonant, the <i>ņ</i> is not undone in
step 6: <i>riekstiņš</i>, <i>riekstiņi</i>, <i>riekstiņu</i> &rarr;
<i>riekstiņ</i>, also the vocative <i>riekstiņ</i>.  <i>izmaiņa</i> and
<i>pārmaiņa</i> are not diminutives, nor are <i>ziņa</i> and its prefixed
forms (<i>apziņa</i>, <i>paziņot</i>), <i>atmiņa</i>, <i>piemiņa</i> and
<i>tūliņ</i>, where <i>-iņ-</i> is part of the root; they are folded like
other words (<i>paziņoja</i>, <i>paziņot</i> &rarr; <i>pazin</i>).
<dt><i>-īš-</i>
<dd>if in <i>p1</i> and after a consonant, it is the <i>-īt-</i> of the
diminutive before <i>-a -i -u</i> and becomes <i>-īt-</i> again:
<i>brālītis</i>, <i>brālīša</i>, <i>brālīši</i>, <i>brālīšu</i> &rarr;
<i>brālīt</i>.  Step 6 would otherwise make it <i>brālīs</i>.  The
reflexive and relative future of verbs in <i>-īšos</i>, <i>-īšot</i>
(<i>klausīšos</i>) has the same letters and is left as <i>klausīt</i>,
apart from <i>klausīties</i> &rarr; <i>klaus</i>.
</dl>

<p>
Reducing diminutives to the base (<i>-iņ-</i>, <i>-īt-</i>, <i>-īš-</i>
removed, <i>c</i>, <i>dz</i> before <i>-iņ-</i> back to <i>k</i>,
<i>g</i>) was tried and merged <i>sieriņš</i> with <i>siers</i> and
<i>maizīte</i> with <i>maize</i>, but also <i>soliņš</i> (a bench) with
<i>solīt</i> (to promise), <i>mītiņš</i> (a rally) with <i>mīts</i> and
<i>daļiņa</i> with <i>dalīt</i>.  The diminutive <i>-ēn-</i>
(<i>kaķēns</i>) is left alone as well: removing it merged
<i>skolēns</i> with <i>skola</i>, and <i>velēna</i>, <i>domēns</i> with
unrelated words.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: consonant alternations after the ending</h3>

<p>
If step 3 found no diminutive and step 2 changed nothing, then after
<i>-a -i -u -us -os -iem</i> the alternations of declensions II, V and VI
are undone, as in the light stemmer: labial + <i>j</i> &rarr; labial
(<i>zivju</i> &rarr; <i>ziv</i>), and after <i>-u</i>, <i>-us</i> also
<i>kš</i> &rarr; <i>kst</i> (<i>dzirkšu</i> &rarr; <i>dzirkst</i>) and
<i>ņņ</i> &rarr; <i>nn</i>.  The palatal consonants are undone in step 6.
</p>

<h3>Step 5: participles and the verb stem</h3>

<p>
<i>-oš-</i>, then <i>-uš-</i>, then <i>-am-</i> or <i>-ām-</i> is removed
if in <i>p2</i> (<i>strādājošs</i>, <i>strādājušais</i>,
<i>redzams</i>, <i>lasāms</i>), and then the <i>-j-</i> of the present and
past stem after a type vowel, if the type vowel is in <i>p2</i>
(<i>strādāja</i>, <i>strādājis</i>, <i>strādājošs</i> &rarr;
<i>strād</i>).  <i>-ēj-</i> is kept in adjectives formed from adverbs
and nouns (<i>vidējs</i>, <i>vietējs</i>, <i>kopējs</i>, <i>pēdējs</i>,
<i>vispārējs</i>, ...), which would otherwise meet <i>vide</i>,
<i>vieta</i>, <i>kopā</i>, and <i>-āj-</i> in agent nouns in
<i>-tājs</i> (<i>vadītājs</i>, <i>ražotājs</i>).
</p>

<p>
The <i>-ts</i>, <i>-ta</i> past passive participles are not removed: they
behave like adjectives, and removing <i>-āt-</i>, <i>-īt-</i>, <i>-ot-</i>
merges <i>salāti</i> with <i>sala</i> and <i>rezultāts</i> with other
<i>rezult-</i> words.
</p>

<h3>Step 6: š, ž, ļ, ņ, č</h3>

<p>
If an ending was removed in step 1 and step 3 found no diminutive, a
final <i>ļ ņ č</i> becomes <i>l n
c</i>, a final <i>šņ žņ šļ žļ ļņ ļļ</i> becomes <i>sn zn sl zl ln ll</i>
and a final <i>š ž</i> becomes <i>s z</i>.  The light stemmer does the
first two only after <i>-a -i -u -us -os -iem</i>, so <i>gaļa</i> &rarr;
<i>gal</i> but <i>gaļas</i> &rarr; <i>gaļ</i>, and <i>eļļa</i> &rarr;
<i>ell</i> but <i>eļļas</i> &rarr; <i>eļļ</i>; here they give <i>gal</i>
and <i>ell</i>.  <i>š</i>
comes from <i>s</i> or <i>t</i> and <i>ž</i> from <i>z</i> or <i>d</i>, so
folding to <i>s</i>, <i>z</i> merges <i>lasis</i>/<i>laša</i>,
<i>interese</i>/<i>interešu</i>, <i>reize</i>/<i>reižu</i>.  The one
common <i>t</i> case is <i>-ieš-</i>, which becomes <i>-iet-</i> if at
least 3 letters precede it (<i>sieviete</i>/<i>sieviešu</i>,
<i>vīrietis</i>/<i>vīriešu</i>).  Doing this only after the endings which
cause the alternation was measured too and was worse, because it splits
adjectives whose <i>š</i> is their own (<i>tieša</i> &rarr; <i>ties</i>
but <i>tiešs</i> &rarr; <i>tieš</i>).
</p>

<p>
Stems ending in <i>tieš tumš gaiš augš plaš sveš droš mīkš kauš biež mež
lauž</i>, and the stems <i>duš</i> and <i>māš</i>, keep their <i>š</i>,
<i>ž</i>: otherwise <i>tiešs</i> would meet <i>tiesa</i>, <i>tumšs</i>
<i>tumsa</i>, <i>gaišs</i> <i>gaiss</i>, <i>bieži</i> <i>biezs</i>, and
<i>māšu</i> (of <i>māte</i>) <i>māsa</i>.
</p>

<p>
Uninflected words keep their letters: <i>dēļ</i> does not become
<i>dēl</i> (<i>dēls</i>).
</p>

<h3>What is not done</h3>

<ul>
<li>Krēsliņš' derivational suffix steps (<i>-šan-</i>, <i>-niek-</i>,
    <i>-isk-</i>, <i>-um-</i>, <i>-īb-</i>, <i>-īg-</i>, <i>-ācij-</i>
    &rarr; <i>-āc-</i>, ...).  They merge words of one root, but also
    many unrelated ones (<i>tikšanās</i>/<i>tikai</i>,
    <i>zemnieks</i>/<i>zems</i>), and the measured gain did not justify
    it.</li>
<li>The negation prefix <i>ne-</i>.  The treebank lemmatizes
    <i>nestrādā</i> as <i>strādāt</i>, so removing it scores well, but
    for search it merges opposites (<i>nelabprāt</i>/<i>labprāt</i>).</li>
<li>Stem changes of the first conjugation (<i>teikt</i>/<i>teic</i>,
    <i>braukt</i>/<i>brauc</i>, <i>notikt</i>/<i>notiek</i>), suppletion
    (<i>būt</i>/<i>ir</i>, <i>iet</i>/<i>gāja</i>) and the conditional of
    verbs other than <i>-ēt</i> verbs (<i>nodrošinātu</i>).</li>
<li>A stopword list.  Some function words equal the stem of a noun or
    adjective (<i>zem</i> and <i>zeme</i>, <i>kur</i> and
    <i>kurš</i>); that needs a stopword list rather than a suffix
    rule.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="evaluation">Evaluation</h2>

<p>
The rules were chosen on the word forms of UD Latvian-LVTB r2.18 (CC BY-SA
4.0) grouped by lemma, using Paice's understemming (UI) and overstemming
(OI) indices, counting each form-lemma pair once, with proper nouns left
out.  The lemmas were split by hash into a tuning set (80%), used while
choosing rules, and a held-out set (20%), measured only at the end.  Each rule
was judged by how much it lowered UI on the tuning set against how many
pairs of different lemmas it merged, and by reading the pairs it merged.
</p>

<table style="border-collapse: separate; border-spacing: 1em 0">
<tr><th></th><th>UI tune</th><th>OI tune</th><th>UI held-out</th><th>OI held-out</th></tr>
<tr><td>no stemming</td><td>1.0000</td><td>6.96e-07</td><td>1.0000</td><td>6.09e-07</td></tr>
<tr><td>light stemmer</td><td>0.5956</td><td>1.26e-05</td><td>0.5657</td><td>1.09e-05</td></tr>
<tr><td>this algorithm</td><td>0.4262</td><td>3.41e-05</td><td>0.3715</td><td>2.61e-05</td></tr>
<tr><td>truncation to 5 letters</td><td>0.4476</td><td>1.53e-04</td><td>0.3979</td><td>1.62e-04</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
Over all lemmas, UI goes from 0.838 to 0.641 for verbs, from 0.303 to
0.032 for adjectives and from 0.050 to 0.023 for nouns.  Most of the new
merges of different lemmas are words of one root (<i>darbs</i> and
<i>darboties</i>, <i>gatavs</i> and <i>gatavoties</i>); the unrelated
ones among the most frequent are <i>paziņot</i>/<i>pazīt</i>,
<i>protams</i>/<i>proti</i>, <i>tikties</i>/<i>ticēt</i> and
<i>mesties</i>/<i>mēs</i>.
</p>

